{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_042948c66e009c810169bb2f62fc20819192c86277916715cd", "created": 1773875043, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Care Coordination Tools for Telehealth and Emergencies (Phone, Contact Information List, Refrigerator)\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on practical tools and artifacts used to support patient care coordination—especially during telehealth visits and emergencies. A CONTACT INFORMATION LIST (a maintained care-team and clinical-context directory) is positioned as the primary coordination asset, with explicit guidance to store it on a PHONE for convenient access and to post it on a REFRIGERATOR for high-visibility, household-wide emergency retrieval. In parallel, TELEHEALTH VISIT guidance emphasizes that a patient can include another person for remote support during medical discussions, reinforcing the theme of enabling participation and timely communication across care scenarios. [Data: Entities (169, 167, 168, 2897, 2913); Relationships (227, 228, 229, 5355, 7590)]\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"The Contact Information List is the central coordination artifact linking routine care and emergencies\",\"explanation\":\"The CONTACT INFORMATION LIST is described as a compiled, maintained record of phone numbers and other contact details for everyone involved in a patient’s care, including the full care team, relevant clinicians, and the patient’s primary care provider (PCP). It is explicitly intended to support emergency response and ongoing care coordination. [Data: Entities (169); Relationships (229)]\\n\\nBeyond being a directory, the list may also include key clinical context (e.g., cancer type, treatment details, and relevant dates) to support timely updates and communication with the PCP, making it both a communications tool and a concise clinical handoff aid. [Data: Entities (169)]\"},{\"summary\":\"A Phone is positioned as the most convenient, always-available access point for the contact list and urgent communication\",\"explanation\":\"PHONE is characterized as a personal communication device used to support care coordination, with guidance to store contact information such as a care-team contact list so it is quickly and conveniently available when needed. The relationships explicitly advise adding the care team contact list to the phone, reinforcing phone-based accessibility as a key operational behavior. [Data: Entities (167); Relationships (227, 7590)]\\n\\nThe PHONE is also identified as a tool for communicating with the care team (including calling triage for urgent concerns) and as a method for joining medical visits remotely by phone. This creates a functional bridge between routine check-ins, urgent escalation, and telehealth participation. [Data: Entities (167)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Posting the contact list on the refrigerator creates household-level redundancy for emergency access\",\"explanation\":\"The REFRIGERATOR is identified as a common, highly visible household location where the patient is advised to hang or post the care-team contact list so it is easy to find quickly, particularly during an emergency. This guidance implies that visibility and shared access (including by others in the household) are critical design considerations in emergency readiness. [Data: Entities (168); Relationships (228)]\\n\\nThe explicit linkage between the CONTACT INFORMATION LIST and EMERGENCY emphasizes that physical placement in an easily accessible location is intended to enable rapid use under time pressure, complementing digital storage on the phone with a resilient, low-tech backup. [Data: Relationships (229, 228); Entities (169, 168)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community emphasizes supported participation in telehealth visits via an additional person joining remotely\",\"explanation\":\"TELEHEALTH VISIT is defined as a remote clinical visit that someone can join by phone or remotely to support the patient during medical discussions. This normalizes the inclusion of a support person and implicitly improves comprehension, advocacy, and recall during complex medical conversations. [Data: Entities (2897)]\\n\\nA specific relationship notes that the patient is advised that someone can join them for telehealth visits to provide support, reinforcing a care model that includes the patient’s broader support network in clinical communication workflows. [Data: Relationships (5355); Entities (2897)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The contact information concept appears both as a specific list artifact and as a generalized instruction to maintain and add contacts\",\"explanation\":\"In addition to the CONTACT INFORMATION LIST entity, the dataset also includes a CONTACT INFORMATION entity, and there is direct guidance to add the contact list to the phone. This suggests the community treats “contact information” both as an abstract need (having contacts available) and as a concrete maintained artifact (the list). [Data: Entities (2913, 169, 167); Relationships (7590, 227)]\\n\\nThe CONTACT INFORMATION LIST is recommended to be kept current and regularly updated, indicating that maintaining accuracy (not only initial creation) is an operational requirement for effective coordination and emergency response. [Data: Entities (169)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Accessibility and speed of retrieval are the core operational goals across artifacts and settings\",\"explanation\":\"Across the PHONE and REFRIGERATOR placements, the repeated theme is ensuring contact details are quickly accessible when needed, especially during emergencies. The CONTACT INFORMATION LIST is recommended to be kept in an easily accessible location for rapid use during an EMERGENCY, while the refrigerator placement is specifically chosen for visibility and ease of finding. [Data: Relationships (229, 228); Entities (168, 169)]\\n\\nSimilarly, storing the care-team contact list on the phone is framed as a convenience and readiness measure, enabling rapid connection to the care team and supporting remote visit participation. Together, these reinforce a redundancy strategy: digital portability (phone) plus physical visibility (refrigerator). [Data: Relationships (227, 228); Entities (167, 168, 169)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Safety and compliance considerations exist in the periphery via MRI restrictions on phones\",\"explanation\":\"The PHONE description includes an explicit safety constraint: it is a metal/electronic object that must be kept out of an MRI imaging room due to magnetic safety restrictions. While not directly connected via relationships to other entities here, it represents an important compliance/safety note within the community’s broader guidance around using phones in care contexts. [Data: Entities (167)]\"}],\"rating\":2.3,\"rating_explanation\":\"Impact severity is low because the community describes benign care-coordination practices (contact lists, phone use, telehealth support) with limited inherent risk beyond routine safety considerations.\"}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1286, "prompt_tokens": 2784, "total_tokens": 4070, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}